{"checks": ["nonempty detailed sections", "current goal revision", "actual supplied quote checks"], "content_contract_checked": true, "content_type": "DETAILED_RESULT", "goal_id": "R1", "goal_scope": "USER_REQUEST", "result": {"_provider_usage": {"input_tokens": 4544, "output_tokens": 704}, "answer": "Plan A and Plan B present a clear tradeoff between cost efficiency and performance reliability under load. Plan A costs $120 per month, offers a recovery checkpoint every 10 seconds, and supports up to 40 concurrent requests using 800 MB of RAM. In contrast, Plan B is significantly cheaper at $75 per month, uses only 500 MB of RAM, but has a slower recovery frequency (every 30 seconds) and a lower capacity limit of 24 concurrent requests.

The evidence from the stress observation ledger highlights that while both plans perform adequately within their declared capacities (Trials 01-16), Plan B fails when pushed beyond its 24-request limit. Starting at Trial 17 (25 active requests), Plan B saturates, completing only 960 requests regardless of increased load, whereas Plan A continues to scale linearly, completing all expected requests up to its 40-request limit. Additionally, Plan B exhibits higher latency (p95 507 ms in Trial 17) compared to Plan A (p95 408 ms) once saturation begins. Therefore, the tradeoff is between Plan B's lower cost and resource usage versus Plan A's superior reliability, faster recovery, and ability to handle higher concurrency without saturation.", "applied_revision": 1, "sections": [{"id": "cost_and_specs", "text": "Plan A costs $120/month with 10s recovery and 40 concurrent request capacity (800 MB RAM). Plan B costs $75/month with 30s recovery and 24 concurrent request capacity (500 MB RAM)."}, {"id": "evidence_from_ledger", "text": "Stress tests show Plan B saturates above 24 requests. In Trial 17, Plan B completed only 960 requests with higher latency, while Plan A handled the load fully."}, {"id": "tradeoffs", "text": "Plan B is cost-effective for low-load scenarios but risks data loss gaps due to slower recovery and performance degradation under saturation. Plan A provides robustness and higher throughput at a premium price."}], "source_claims": [{"quote": "Plan A: cost 120 dollars per month; recovery checkpoint every 10 seconds; handles 40 concurrent requests; uses 800 MB host RAM.", "revision": 1, "source_id": "D1"}, {"quote": "Plan B: cost 75 dollars per month; checkpoint every 30 seconds; handles 24 concurrent requests; uses 500 MB.", "revision": 1, "source_id": "D1"}, {"quote": "Trial 17: 25 active requests; Plan A p95 408 ms, peak RSS 761 MB, completed 1000 requests; Plan B p95 507 ms, peak RSS 474 MB, completed 960 requests. Plan B saturated above declared capacity.", "revision": 1, "source_id": "D1"}]}, "revision": 1, "stimulus_id": "R1", "text": "Plan A and Plan B present a clear tradeoff between cost efficiency and performance reliability under load. Plan A costs $120 per month, offers a recovery checkpoint every 10 seconds, and supports up to 40 concurrent requests using 800 MB of RAM. In contrast, Plan B is significantly cheaper at $75 per month, uses only 500 MB of RAM, but has a slower recovery frequency (every 30 seconds) and a lower capacity limit of 24 concurrent requests.

The evidence from the stress observation ledger highlights that while both plans perform adequately within their declared capacities (Trials 01-16), Plan B fails when pushed beyond its 24-request limit. Starting at Trial 17 (25 active requests), Plan B saturates, completing only 960 requests regardless of increased load, whereas Plan A continues to scale linearly, completing all expected requests up to its 40-request limit. Additionally, Plan B exhibits higher latency (p95 507 ms in Trial 17) compared to Plan A (p95 408 ms) once saturation begins. Therefore, the tradeoff is between Plan B's lower cost and resource usage versus Plan A's superior reliability, faster recovery, and ability to handle higher concurrency without saturation."}
